# WHAT AND WHY. Stock Chrome 151, asked for ICE candidates with no STUN servers, answers with a
# host candidate whose address is a random UUID hostname -- `<uuid>.local`, RFC 6762/6763 mDNS
# concealment, feature kWebRtcHideLocalIpsWithMdns (enabled by default,
# third_party/blink/common/features.cc:2746). This engine answers with the raw address --
# measured on the shipping 1001 binary: one host candidate, `198.18.0.1` in the clear where
# Chrome shows `<uuid>.local` (Veil/docs/MIGRATION-UNGOOGLED.md 6.7, filed there as T03's
# "Cause 3"). Two tells in one: a Chrome-branded UA that can never produce a `.local` candidate
# is itself a population-distribution tell; and any policy that does gather host candidates
# leaks an address Chrome would have hidden.
#
# WHY NOT THE GN ARG. The upstream mechanism is a buildflag: ungoogled-chromium's flags.gn sets
# enable_mdns=false and the tree compiles the responder out -- MdnsResponderAdapter is
# constructed behind `BUILDFLAG(ENABLE_MDNS)` (peer_connection_dependency_factory.cc:774),
# NetworkContext::CreateMdnsResponder is NOTREACHED without it (network_context.cc:2626), and
# services/network plus net/dns drop mdns_responder.cc / mdns_client*.cc from their sources
# lists. Restoring the arg compiles ~10 files back in and re-enables chrome/browser/
# local_discovery with it (chrome/common/features.gni:65 wires enable_service_discovery straight
# off enable_mdns). And the responder is not just an obfuscator: MdnsResponder::
# CreateNameForAddress sends real mDNS ANNOUNCE packets on every network interface -- two per
# name, RFC 6762 s.8.3 (services/network/mdns_responder.cc:1190-1233) --
# MdnsResponderManager::StartIfNeeded binds a UDP 5353 server socket per interface
# (mdns_responder.cc:849), and the service answers multicast queries and probe/conflict
# detection. For a local-first fingerprint browser that is new background networking and a new
# machine-shaped surface (the name-generator TXT record enumerates every name in use), all to
# obtain a string a page can never check by resolving -- the harness says as much:
# `webrtc.candidates.*.addressSha256` is excluded from assertion 2 because Chromium randomises
# these hostnames per session and origin, so the VALUE is not the fingerprint; the CLASS is.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES INSTEAD. Same observable, none of the machinery. `Port::AddAddress` -- the one
# door every host candidate walks through -- intercepts local candidates and rewrites the
# address to a synthetic `<uuid>.local` hostname. The uuid comes from webrtc's own
# `CreateRandomUuid` (rtc_base/crypto_random.h), the generator the real responder's
# RandomUuidNameGenerator uses (mdns_responder.cc:109); the `.local` suffix is the same
# LOCAL_TLD stock Chrome's candidates carry (p2p_constants.h:54). Names are memoised per
# process: stable per address for the process' lifetime -- a page sees the same name across
# re-gathers, as the responder's refcounted names give -- and fresh per launch, so no
# cross-session correlation. `related_address` is emptied exactly as MaybeObfuscateAddress's
# callback does (port.cc:276, `copy.set_related_address(SocketAddress())`), because a hostname
# candidate with the raw IP in raddr is the leak wearing a disguise.
#
# TWO HALVES, ONE PATCH. The hostname rewrite alone is not enough: the session's sanitize path
# re-derives the address at signaling time. PortAllocator::SanitizeCandidate
# (port_allocator.cc:315) computes `use_hostname_address = (is_local || is_prflx) &&
